image_alt: "A random die"
---

A couple months ago, I engaged my friend in a debate on arbitrariness. Our discussion centered on 
the rationality of age of consent; He argues that the age of consent policy has no scientific backing
as it was dependent on personal references and culture, not logic. I argue that the age of consent 
being 18 makes moral sense to me and reflects maturity without compromising autonomy —my only argument
was that 18 years of age is rational to a degree. His point remains every decision that amounted to 
the declaration of a figure as the age of maturity was chosen based on intuitions and fallacious reasoning,
else countries like Argentina would be sanctioned for their age of consent (13).

The *Merriam-Webster* dictionary defines arbitrary as: *“Chosen, decided, etc. seemingly at random or on a
whim rather than in a reasoned and methodical way”.*

![line image]({{ site.baseurl }}/assets/images/line.jpeg)

We both had a point. The fact is multiple policies, cultures and decisions around the world are executed just
because. Take the lines of latitude and longitude, two imaginary lines but different origins. The lines of 
latitude are not arbitrary, for it is based on the physical shape and rotation of the earth, for the equator 
cannot be assumed. It exists. It is absolute. In contrast, the lines of longitude were not based on any scientific
research nor any existing physical axioms; the *Greenwich Meridian* (0°) was chosen because of convenience via
voting in 1884 at the *International Meridian Conference, Washington, D.C.*, in fact, France refused to adopt 
it and stuck to its local meridian till 1911.

Maps and borders are generally considered arbitrary when considering their origins, this being the reason why the
concept of xenophobia eludes me. Take India for example; it has a large land area and population, and due to 
hand-drawn border drawings by *Cyril Radcliffe* in 1947 —about 79 years ago— made a people who once shared culture,
people with same phenotype, morphology, language and culture see themselves as enemies —although political shift of
power and constraints played a significant role.

![soldiers]({{ site.baseurl }}/assets/images/guys.jpeg)

A Bangladeshi man must obtain a visa to gain access to a country which once accessed, he cannot be distinguished from
its citizens, because a man who does not reside there —nor do his descendants—drew a line. 

The mandatory power of a passport was first introduced during WWI as a temporary means to identify members of a state.
It then became a means to prove citizenship and mitigate **spies**. In today’s world a passport has been delegated 
strength and this depends on the perceived strength of the issuing country and its relationship with the country being
visited.

![Map image]({{ site.baseurl }}/assets/images/map.jpeg)

The role of a passport has slowly shifted as a temporary solution to prevent spies and identify citizenship to a form
of **human herding** via border control. The criteria that determines if another human is allowed into another region 
on the Earth’s crust should not be based on poorly drawn lines.

I sometimes fall asleep at night saying to myself saying *“The age of consent is not a universally agreed chorological
rationale and is because a group of individuals thought so, the lines of longitude s are not absolute and I cannot
travel to Australia without a visa for some individuals decided my borders are weak”.

> written by Gabriel O.  for **The Commoner Archive 1st of August**, 2026
